  "query_preview": "..\n(This evening through next Friday)\n\nWEATHER PATTERN OVERVIEW: The upper level ridge situated over the\nfour corners continue to bring near record heat in most of central\nand northern Nevada today with major risk for heat-related\nillnesses as temperatures remain in the upper 90s to 100s. Various\nheat related products are still in effect until 11 PM PDT Sunday\nacross all zones except for the Excessive Heat Warning for eastern\nElko County ending at 11 PM PDT Monday as overnight lows remain in\nthe upper 70s combined with high temperatures around 100 degrees.\n\nThe upper ridge pattern is bringing in moisture over Arizona and\nthe Baja that is moving northward into Nevada. Active monsoon\nweather pattern returns across much of central and parts of\nnortheastern Nevada this weekend as PWAT values increase up to 0.8\nto 1 inch, along with CAPE values increasing above 200 J/kg.\nToday, much of the thunderstorms are expected to be dry, however\nthe slow motion of these storms may contribute with some wetting\nrains reaching the valleys. Tomorrow, a more mix of wet/dry\nthunderstorms expected across much of central Nevada with dry\nthunderstorm reaching into northeastern Nevada. Storm motion is\nexpected to be a little fast early in the day, but by the\nafternoon, the motion will slow down, allowing the potential for\nmore rain reaching the valleys. Throughout the next week, the\nactive monsoon pattern is expected to continue each afternoon with\na mix of wet/dry thunderstorms across central and northeastern\nNevada. As for Humboldt County, PWAT values increase up to 1 inch,\nhowever little to no instability is leading to little to no chance\nof active weather throughout the week. \n\nElevated fire weather conditions today and tomorrow across\nnorthern Nevada along the state border as winds expected to gust\nup to 20-25 mph with low humidity around 10% and very high\ntemperatures. Red Flag warning is in effect today in central\nNevada until 9 PM this evening due to dry thunderstorms. Over the\nweek, fire weather conditions will begin to ease as the monsoonal\nflow will continue to bring moisture, causing minimum relative\nhumidity to be 20% or above across the region by the end of the\nweek. \n\n\nFORECAST CONFIDENCE & DEVIATIONS: High confidence of near record\ntemperatures over central and northern Nevada this afternoon and\ntomorrow. Moderate confidence of elevated fire weather conditions\ndue to hot, dry, and breezy winds over northern Nevada today and\ntomorrow. High confidence of Red Flag conditions this afternoon\nand evening over central Nevada due to dry thunderstorms. Made\nchanges to the POP and weather grids to better reflect the active\nmonsoon pattern over central and northeastern Nevada.",
